<body>

<h1>ArgusNet &mdash; Current Application Features</h1>
<p class="subtitle">This document describes every feature that is already built and working inside ArgusNet as of today.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">1. Dashboard &mdash; Main Monitoring View</p>

<p class="feature-title">1.1 &nbsp; Targets Table</p>
<p class="row">The dashboard shows a full-screen table listing every monitored device. For each target the table displays:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Name</b></td><td>The friendly name given to the device (e.g. &ldquo;Core Switch&rdquo;).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>IP Address</b></td><td>The IP or hostname that is being monitored, shown as a styled code badge.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Status</b></td><td>Online / Offline badge. Green for Online, red for Offline, grey if never pinged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Latency</b></td><td>The response time of the most recent ping in milliseconds.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Avg Latency</b></td><td>Average response time across all historical pings.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Uptime %</b></td><td>Percentage of successful pings with a color-coded progress bar (green &ge;99%, yellow &ge;90%, red below).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Loss %</b></td><td>Percentage of failed pings out of all pings recorded.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Last Check</b></td><td>Timestamp of the last ping attempt.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Actions</b></td><td>Buttons: Ping (manual), Chart, Edit, Delete.</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="feature-title">1.2 &nbsp; Stats Bar</p>
<p class="row">Four summary cards at the top of the dashboard provide an instant overview:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Card</th><th>What it shows</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Total Targets</b></td><td>Number of devices being monitored.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Online</b></td><td>Count of devices with last status = Online.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Offline</b></td><td>Count of devices with last status = Offline.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Avg Latency</b></td><td>Average response time across all online targets.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">2. Ping Functions</p>

<p class="feature-title">2.1 &nbsp; Manual Ping (Single Target)</p>
<p class="row">Each row has a <b>Ping</b> button. Clicking it sends a ping request to that specific device immediately and updates the row in real time (status, latency, loss%).</p>

<p class="feature-title">2.2 &nbsp; Ping All</p>
<p class="row">A <b>Ping All</b> button at the top of the dashboard pings every target at once in a single API call. All rows update simultaneously when the results return.</p>

<p class="feature-title">2.3 &nbsp; Auto-Refresh (Automatic Polling)</p>
<p class="row">The dashboard has an auto-refresh control with:</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; An <b>On/Off toggle</b> to enable or disable automatic polling.</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; An <b>interval selector</b> (30 s, 1 min, 2 min, 5 min, 10 min).</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; A <b>live countdown timer</b> showing seconds until the next automatic ping.</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; <b>State persistence</b> &mdash; the on/off state, chosen interval, and exact countdown are saved in <span class="tech">localStorage</span> so they survive browser refresh and page navigation.</p>

<p class="feature-title">2.4 &nbsp; Multi-Method Ping Engine (Backend)</p>
<p class="row">The PHP backend tries multiple methods to determine if a host is reachable, in order:</p>
<p class="row">1. <b>Raw ICMP socket</b> &mdash; fastest method, used when the PHP <span class="tech">sockets</span> extension is available.</p>
<p class="row">2. <b>System ping command</b> &mdash; uses the OS <span class="tech">ping</span> binary via <span class="tech">proc_open()</span> (array form, bypasses shell injection). Tries normal ping first, then <span class="tech">sudo&nbsp;-n&nbsp;ping</span> if permissions are missing.</p>
<p class="row">3. <b>TCP port fallback</b> &mdash; if ICMP is blocked, tries connecting to common ports (22, 80, 443, 23, 3389, 445) via <span class="tech">fsockopen()</span>. Useful for servers and routers behind firewalls.</p>
<p class="row">Works on both <b>Windows</b> and <b>Linux servers</b>.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">3. Target Management (CRUD)</p>

<p class="feature-title">3.1 &nbsp; Add Target</p>
<p class="row">A modal dialog lets the user add a new device to monitor. Fields: <b>Name</b> and <b>IP Address / Hostname</b>. Validation is applied on both frontend and backend.</p>

<p class="feature-title">3.2 &nbsp; Edit Target</p>
<p class="row">An <b>Edit</b> button opens a pre-filled modal where the user can update the name or IP address of an existing target.</p>

<p class="feature-title">3.3 &nbsp; Delete Target</p>
<p class="row">A <b>Delete</b> button removes the target and all its ping history from the database after a confirmation prompt.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">4. Response Time Chart</p>

<p class="feature-title">4.1 &nbsp; Chart Modal</p>
<p class="row">Clicking the <b>Chart</b> button on any target opens a modal with a line chart showing the last 60 ping results for that device. Features inside the chart modal:</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; <b>Line chart</b> with response time in milliseconds on the Y-axis and time on the X-axis.</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; <b>Red dots</b> on the chart mark pings where the host was Offline.</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; <b>Average line</b> &mdash; a dashed horizontal reference line showing the average latency.</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; <b>Custom tooltip</b> &mdash; hovering a point shows the exact time and latency (or &ldquo;Offline&rdquo; for failed pings).</p>
<p class="row">&bull; &nbsp; <b>Mini stats bar</b> inside the modal: Uptime %, Avg latency, Min latency, Max latency.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">5. Ping History Page</p>

<p class="feature-title">5.1 &nbsp; History Table</p>
<p class="row">A separate <b>History</b> page (accessible from the top navigation) shows a full paginated log of every ping ever performed, with columns: Target name, IP, Status, Response time, Error message, and Timestamp.</p>

<p class="feature-title">5.2 &nbsp; Filter by Target</p>
<p class="row">A dropdown at the top of the History page filters the log to show only pings for a selected target.</p>

<p class="feature-title">5.3 &nbsp; Pagination</p>
<p class="row">Results are paginated (50 per page) with Previous / Next navigation and a &ldquo;Page X of Y &mdash; Z total&rdquo; counter.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">6. Navigation</p>

<p class="feature-title">6.1 &nbsp; Top Navigation Bar</p>
<p class="row">A fixed top navbar with the <b>ArgusNet</b> brand name and links to <b>Dashboard</b> and <b>History</b>. Active page link is highlighted.</p>

<p class="feature-title">6.2 &nbsp; Client-Side Routing</p>
<p class="row">The app is a Single Page Application (SPA) using React Router. Navigation between Dashboard and History is instant with no full page reload. The auto-refresh timer on the Dashboard is never destroyed when navigating to History and back &mdash; it keeps running in the background using CSS visibility instead of unmounting.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">7. Backend API</p>

<p class="row">The Laravel backend exposes a set of JSON API endpoints consumed by the React frontend:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Method</th><th>Endpoint</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td><span class="tech">/api/targets</span></td><td>Returns all targets with uptime%, avg latency, last status, and last ping time.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td><span class="tech">/api/history</span></td><td>Returns paginated ping history, optionally filtered by target ID.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>GET</td><td><span class="tech">/api/targets/{id}/chart-data</span></td><td>Returns the last 60 ping results for a specific target (used by the chart modal).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td><span class="tech">/targets</span></td><td>Creates a new target.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>PUT</td><td><span class="tech">/targets/{id}</span></td><td>Updates an existing target&rsquo;s name or IP.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>DELETE</td><td><span class="tech">/targets/{id}</span></td><td>Deletes a target and all its history.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td><span class="tech">/targets/{id}/ping</span></td><td>Pings a single target and records the result.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>POST</td><td><span class="tech">/ping-all</span></td><td>Pings all targets and returns all results at once.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">8. Database</p>

<p class="row">The app uses two database tables:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Table</th><th>Key Columns</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>targets</b></td><td>id, name, ip_address, created_at</td><td>Stores monitored devices.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>ping_histories</b></td><td>id, target_id, is_success, response_time, error_message, created_at</td><td>Stores every ping result.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p class="section-title">9. User Interface</p>

<p class="feature-title">9.1 &nbsp; Dark Theme</p>
<p class="row">The entire application uses a dark color scheme inspired by GitHub&rsquo;s dark mode &mdash; dark backgrounds, blue accent color, green/red status indicators. All colors are defined as CSS variables for easy customization.</p>

<p class="feature-title">9.2 &nbsp; Responsive Layout</p>
<p class="row">The layout uses CSS Grid and Flexbox. The stats bar, table, and chart modal are all responsive and work at different screen widths.</p>

<p class="feature-title">9.3 &nbsp; Technology Stack</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Technology</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Backend framework</td><td>Laravel 11 (PHP)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Frontend framework</td><td>React 18 (JSX)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Build tool</td><td>Vite 7 with laravel-vite-plugin</td></tr>
  <tr><td>CSS</td><td>Tailwind CSS v4 + custom CSS variables</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Charts</td><td>Recharts library</td></tr>
  <tr><td>HTTP client (frontend)</td><td>Axios</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Routing (frontend)</td><td>React Router v6</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Icons</td><td>Font Awesome 6 (CDN)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Database</td><td>SQLite / MySQL (Laravel Eloquent ORM)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Ping methods</td><td>Raw ICMP socket, system ping binary, TCP fsockopen</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="footer">ArgusNet &mdash; Current features as of May 2026. For planned features see ArgusNet_Production_Features.doc</p>

</body>
</html>
